※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A **lifetime name** means "as long as whatever else in this signature carries
※ it": `⧖x` written on several types in one signature means they all live as
※ long as each other, and an answer carrying `⧖x` lives as long as the shortest
※ of them.  One parameter carrying it is the ordinary case; several is what no
※ shorter form could say, since then neither of them alone is true of the answer.
※
※ **The name comes after `⧖`** and stands where `static` would, the two being
※ the same slot answering the same question: `&mut ⧖x u32`.  U+29D6 WHITE
※ HOURGLASS is the one glyph in Unicode that means "how long", and it is one
※ character, so it has no ASCII substitute and claims nothing.
※
※ **A lifetime is declared by being used**, exactly as a type parameter is.
※ There is no list at the head of the signature to write and none to keep in
※ step; what makes a name mean something is that it stands in a parameter's
※ type, and a name that stands in none is refused (4567).
※
※ **The caller works out the rest, as before**: the answer lasts as long as the
※ program only where *every* argument carrying the name did, that being the only
※ promise that holds whichever one the body picked.
※
※ Rust writes this as `fn f<'a>(b: &'a mut u32, c: &'a mut u32) -> &'a mut u32`,
※ which is the same idea with the name declared at the head and a leading tick.
※ The tick is not available here -- it begins a character literal, and written
※ after a name it is already the type-parameter mark -- and declaring at the head
※ is what the generics decision already turned down.

let top: mut u32 = 0u32
let other: mut u32 = 0u32

※ The answer comes from either parameter, so nothing about one of them alone is
※ true of it and one name on all three is.
fn either(a: bool, b: &mut ⧖x u32, c: &mut ⧖x u32) → &mut ⧖x u32:
    if a:
        b
    else:
        c

※ One parameter carrying the name, which is the ordinary case.
fn first(v: &⧖y u32) → &⧖y u32:
    v

※ A name on a parameter and not on the answer is allowed and says nothing: what
※ makes a promise is the answer carrying it.
fn reads(v: &⧖z u32) → u32:
    v⌖

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ both arguments last as long as the program, so the answer does
    let kept: &mut static u32 = either(true, &mut top, &mut other)
    kept⌖ ← 9u32
    ※ the other arm, and one whose arguments do not
    let n: mut u32 = 1u32
    let m: mut u32 = 2u32
    let short: &mut u32 = either(false, &mut n, &mut m)
    short⌖ ← 7u32
    ※ mixed: one argument lasts and one does not, so the answer does not
    let q: mut u32 = 3u32
    let another: u32 = 4u32
    let mixed: &mut u32 = either(true, &mut top, &mut q)
    ※ one name on one parameter, which is the ordinary case.  Each
    ※ of these places is lent once: `n` and `q` are already out above
    let plain: u32 = 1u32
    let r: &u32 = first(&plain)
    let lasting: &static u32 = first(&other)
    if top ≠ 9u32:
        1u6
    elif short⌖ ≠ 7u32:
        2u6
    elif mixed⌖ ≠ 9u32:
        3u6
    elif r⌖ ≠ 1u32:
        4u6
    elif lasting⌖ ≠ 0u32:
        5u6
    elif reads(&another) ≠ 4u32:
        6u6
    else:
        0u6
